  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1234 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nEarly afternoon water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air\nanalysis reveal longwave ridging remains in place from the \nsouthern plains to the northern plains westward through the \nIntermountain West. At the surface, weak high pressure is spread\nout over nearly all areas east of the Rockies, leading to a \nnebulous pressure gradient across southwest KS. Short range \nguidance indicates 850-mb warming over the central plains will \nonly be around 1 degree C despite large-scale subsidence ahead \nof the ridge, which will translate to afternoon highs inching up\ninto the low to mid 90s. Similar to the last several days, \nstrong solar insolation will support deep boundary layer mixing \nby early/mid afternoon, fostering another round of weak, \ntransient light rain showers that will pose little threat other \nthan a lightning strike or two. These will quickly dissipate \nthis evening as sunset approaches, and the remainder of the \nevening and overnight period will be quiet with Monday morning \nlows dropping into the upper 60s to mid 70s.\n\nDaytime Monday, short range ensembles agree the upper level ridge \nwill begin to creep southeastward towards the central plains. While \nthis evolution is subtle, the increase in subsidence aloft should \nengender more substantial warming at 850-mb, and therefore result in \na larger jump in afternoon temperatures as highs reach into the \nupper 90s/near 100. \n\nTuesday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nsuggest the upper level ridge will rotate anticyclonically first \nfrom near the central Rockies into the central plains, then \nsouthward into the southern plains, and finally westward to \nnear the Four Corner region. The end result of this synoptic \nprogression will be continued afternoon temperatures in the 90s \nto low 100s, as well as the return of precipitation chances \nfavoring our northern zones beginning Wednesday evening.",
